ley Graphs of Finite Abelian Groups}, author={Bela Bajnok and W. Kyle Beatty}, year={2024}, eprint={2406.04045}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={math.CO}, doi={https://doi.org/10.2140/cnt.2025.14.1}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.04045}, }--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSpectral Sidorenko inequalities and edge-spectral supersaturation (https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.26614)\nciteturn4academia13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5 months ago; Authors: Yongtao Li, Wilson Lin, Hong Liu, Shengtong Zhang ... We prove that Sidorenko's conjecture is equivalent to a spectral strengthening: \\[ \\hom(H,G)\\ge M(G)^e |V(G)|^{v-2e} \\quad \\text{ if and only if }\\quad \\hom(H,G)\\ge λ(G)^{2e-v}M(G)^{v-e}. ... As an application, we obtain sharp asymptotic edge-spectral supersaturation results for complete bipartite graphs and even cycles.\nTitle: Spectral Sidorenko inequalities and edge-spectral supersaturation\nAuthors: Yongtao Li, Wilson Lin, Hong Liu, Shengtong Zhang\nDate: Tue May 26 06:51:18 2026\n\nWe develop a spectral approach to Sidorenko-type inequalities and apply it to establish sharp edge-spectral supersaturation results. Let $H$ be a bipartite graph with $v$ vertices and $e$ edges, where $v\\le e$, and write $M(G)=2e(G)$. We prove that Sidorenko's conjecture is equivalent to a spectral strengthening: \\[ \\hom(H,G)\\ge M(G)^e |V(G)|^{v-2e} \\quad \\text{ if and only if }\\quad \\hom(H,G)\\ge λ(G)^{2e-v}M(G)^{v-e}. \\] We also introduce an operator-norm certificate which, via the Riesz--Thorin interpolation, gives direct proofs of the spectral Sidorenko inequality in several cases. The converse direction in the equivalence theorem is proved by a tensor-power spectral regularization lemma. As an application, we obtain sharp asymptotic edge-spectral supersaturation results for complete bipartite graphs and even cycles. Let $S_{t-1,m}$ be the split graph with $m$ edges obtained by joining a clique $K_{t-1}$ with an independent set. For any $m$-edge graph $G$ with $λ(G)>λ(S_{t-1,m})$, $$\\texttt{#} K_{t,t}(G) \\ge \\Big(\\frac{2^{-(t-1)^2}}{(t!)^2}-o(1)\\Big)m^t \\quad \\text{and}\\quad \\texttt{#}C_{2t}(G) \\ge \\Big(\\frac{(t-1)!}{2t^t}-o(1)\\Big)m^t.$$ Both constants are best possible: the first is attained asymptotically by random graphs, while the second is attained by split graphs. The supersaturation proofs combine spectral Sidorenko inequalities with heavy-edge pruning process, a Perron-vector localized/delocalized dichotomy, and incidence-matrix inequalities.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMore on spectral supersaturation for the bowtie (https://www.znakschoola.ru/?_=%2Fpdf%2F2601.04671%23UE7DkG0PaBHZOakR54g1Wl8%3D)\nciteturn4search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8 months ago; In this paper, we studied the supersaturation problem for the bowtie in terms of the spectral ... We solved a conjecture of Li–Feng–Peng [13] by showing that if n is sufficiently large and ... only if G is a complete bipartite graph.\n5\nConcluding remarks\nIn this paper, we studied the supersaturation problem for the bowtie in terms of the spectral\nradius. We solved a conjecture of Li–Feng–Peng [13] by showing that if n is sufficiently large and\nG is an n-vertex graph with λ(G) ≥λ(Kq\n⌈n\n2 ⌉,⌊n\n2 ⌋), where 2 ≤q ≤δ√n for some absolute constant\nδ > 0, then τ(G) ≥\n\u0000q\n2\n\u0001\n⌊n\n2 ⌋, with equality if and only if G = Kq\n⌈n\n2 ⌉,⌊n\n2 ⌋. In fact, by the definition of\nKq\n⌈n\n2 ⌉,⌊n\n2 ⌋, it can be seen that the best possible range for q satisfies q ≤⌊n+1\n4 ⌋. It would therefore\nbe interesting to investigate whether Theorem 1.3 remains valid over this wider range.\nConjecture 5.1. For sufficiently large n and 2 ≤q ≤⌊n+1\n4 ⌋, if G is an n-vertex graph with\nλ(G) ≥λ(Kq\n⌈n\n2 ⌋, with equality if and only if G = Kq\n⌈n\n2 ⌉,⌊n\n2 ⌋), then τ(G) ≥\n\u0000q\n2\n\u0001\n⌊n\n2 ⌉,⌊n\n2 ⌋.\nAt the end of this paper, we conclude another type of spectral extremal problem for graphs\nwith given size m, instead of the order n. A well-known result of Nosal [29] and Nikiforov [23]\nstates that if G is a triangle-free graph with m edges, then λ(G) ≤√m, with equality if and\nonly if G is a complete bipartite graph. In 2023, Ning and Zhai [27] proved that if λ(G) ≥√m,\nthen G contains at least ⌊\n√m−1\n2\n⌋triangles, unless G is a complete bipartite graph. For related\nedge-spectral extremal results, we refer to [15–17] and references therein.\nIn 2023, Li, Lu and Peng [11] proved that for every integer m ≥8, if G is an F2-free graph\nwith m edges, then λ(G) ≤1+√4m−3\n2\n, with equality if and only if G = K2 ∨m−1\n2 K1, which is the\njoin obtained from an edge K2 and an independent set m−1\n2 K1. Consequently, we are led to the\nfollowing supersaturation problem under the edge-spectral condition.\nProblem 5.2. Given an m-edge graph G with λ(G) > 1+√4m−3\n2\n, what is the best possible lower\nbound on the number of copies of the bowtie F2 in G?\nReferences\n[1] J. Balogh, F. C. Clemen, On stability of the Erd˝os–Rademacher problem, Illinois J. Math.\n67 (1) (2023) 1–11.\n[2] B. Bollob´as, V. Nikiforov, Cliques and the spectral radius, J. Combin. Theory Ser. B 97\n(2007) 859–865.\n[3] J. Byrne, D.N. Desai, M. Tait, A general theorem in spectral extremal graph theory, (2024),\nTrans. Amer. Math. Soc., to appear.\n[4] S. Cioab˘a, L. Feng, M. Tait, X.-D. Zhang, The maximum spectral radius of graphs without\nfriendship subgraphs, Electron. J. Combin. 27 (4) (2020), #P4.22.\n[5] S. Cioab˘a, D.N. Desai, M. Tait, A spectral Erd˝os–S´os theorem, SIAM J. Discrete Math. 37\n(3) (2023) 2228–2239.\n[6] P. Erd˝os, On the number of triangles contained in certain graphs, Canad. Math. Bull. 7 (1)\n(1964) 53–56.\n[7] P. Erd˝os, M. Simonovits, Supersaturated graphs and hypergraphs, Combinatorica 3 (1983)\n181–192.\n[8] P. Erd˝os, Z. F¨uredi, R.J. Gould, D.S. Gunderson, Extremal graphs for intersecting triangles,\nJ. Combin. Theory Ser. B 64 (1) (1995) 89–100.\n13--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA spectral Lovász-Simonovits theorem (https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.01709)\nciteturn4academia15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.3 years ago; Authors: Yongtao Li, Lihua Feng, Yuejian Peng ... This result solved a conjecture proposed by Erd\\H{o}s in 1962. ... Let $Y_{n,2,q}$ be the graph obtained from the bipartite Tur\\'{a}n graph $T_{n,2}$ by embedding a matching with $q$ edges into the vertex part of size $\\lceil n/2\\rceil$. Using the supersaturation-stability method and the classical spectral techniques, we firstly prove that for $n\\ge 300q^2$, each graph $G$ on $n$ vertices with $\\lambda (G) \\ge \\lambda (Y_{n,2,q})$ contains at least $q\\lfloor n/2 \\rfloor$ triangles.\nTitle: A spectral Lovász-Simonovits theorem\nAuthors: Yongtao Li, Lihua Feng, Yuejian Peng\nDate: Sat Aug  3 08:27:38 2024\n\nA fundamental result in extremal graph theory attributes to Mantel's theorem, which states that every graph on $n$ vertices with more than $\\lfloor n^2/4 \\rfloor$ edges contains a triangle. About half of a century ago, Lov\\'{a}sz and Simonovits (1975) provided a supersaturation phenomenon, which asserts that for $q< n/2$, every graph with $\\lfloor n^2/4 \\rfloor +q$ edges contains at least $q\\lfloor n/2 \\rfloor$ triangles. This result solved a conjecture proposed by Erd\\H{o}s in 1962. In this paper, we establish a spectral version of the result of Lov\\'{a}sz and Simonovits. Let $Y_{n,2,q}$ be the graph obtained from the bipartite Tur\\'{a}n graph $T_{n,2}$ by embedding a matching with $q$ edges into the vertex part of size $\\lceil n/2\\rceil$. Using the supersaturation-stability method and the classical spectral techniques, we firstly prove that for $n\\ge 300q^2$, each graph $G$ on $n$ vertices with $\\lambda (G) \\ge \\lambda (Y_{n,2,q})$ contains at least $q\\lfloor n/2 \\rfloor$ triangles. Moreover, let $T_{n,2,q}$ be the graph obtained from $T_{n,2}$ by embedding a star with $q$ edges into the vertex part of size $\\lceil n/2\\rceil$. Secondly, we show further that $T_{n,2,q}$ is the unique spectral extremal graph that contains at most $q\\lfloor n/2 \\rfloor$ triangles and attains the maximum of the spectral radius. This result answers a spectral triangle counting problem due to Ning and Zhai (2023). Thirdly, we present an asymptotically spectral stability result under a specific constraint on the triangle covering number. The third result could be regarded as a spectral extension of a recent result proved by Balogh and Clemen (2023), and independently by Liu and Mubayi (2022).--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nΑποτελέσματα αναζήτησης - \"finite abelian groups\" :: Library Catalog (https://vufind2.lib.aegean.gr/EDS/Search?lookfor=%22finite+abelian+groups%22&type=SU)\nciteturn4search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; ## On the diameter of undirected Cayley graphs of finite abelian groups ... Θεματικοί όροι: Extremal problems in graph theory, Finite abelian groups, Mathematics - Number Theory, Inverse problems of additive number theory, including sumsets, Combinatorial aspects of difference sets (number-theoretic, group-theoretic, etc.), Group Theory (math.GR), abelian group, 11B13, Cayley graph, Additive bases, including sumsets, signed sumset, Other combinatorial number theory, FOS: Mathematics, sumset, Mathematics - Combinatorics, Combinatorics (math.CO), Number Theory (math.NT), diameter, Mathematics - Group Theory\n## On the diameter of undirected Cayley graphs of finite abelian groups\n\nΣυγγραφείς: Bajnok, Béla, Beatty, W. Kyle\n\nΠηγή: Combinatorics and Number Theory. 14:1-12\n\nΘεματικοί όροι: Extremal problems in graph theory, Finite abelian groups, Mathematics - Number Theory, Inverse problems of additive number theory, including sumsets, Combinatorial aspects of difference sets (number-theoretic, group-theoretic, etc.), Group Theory (math.GR), abelian group, 11B13, Cayley graph, Additive bases, including sumsets, signed sumset, Other combinatorial number theory, FOS: Mathematics, sumset, Mathematics - Combinatorics, Combinatorics (math.CO), Number Theory (math.NT), diameter, Mathematics - Group Theory\n\nΠεριγραφή αρχείου: application/xml\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMore on Nosal's spectral theorem: Books and $4$-cycles (https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.14366)\nciteturn4academia16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.2 years ago; In this paper, we solve three open problems in spectral extremal graph theory which generalize the classical Tur\\'{a}n-type supersaturation results. (a) We prove that every $m$-edge graph $G$ with the spectral radius $\\lambda (G) > \\sqrt{m}$ contains at least $\\frac{1}{144} \\sqrt{m}$ triangles sharing a common edge. ... This confirms a conjecture of Li, Liu and Feng and unifies a series of spectral extremal results on books and cliques. ... This result settles a problem raised by Ning and Zhai, and it gives the first asymptotics for counting degenerate bipartite graphs.\nTitle: More on Nosal's spectral theorem: Books and $4$-cycles\nAuthors: Yongtao Li, Hong Liu, Shengtong Zhang\nDate: Wed Aug 20 02:46:35 2025\n\nSpectral graph theory studies how the eigenvalues of a graph relate to the structural properties of a graph. In this paper, we solve three open problems in spectral extremal graph theory which generalize the classical Tur\\'{a}n-type supersaturation results. (a) We prove that every $m$-edge graph $G$ with the spectral radius $\\lambda (G) > \\sqrt{m}$ contains at least $\\frac{1}{144} \\sqrt{m}$ triangles sharing a common edge. This result confirms a conjecture of Nikiforov, and Li and Peng. Moreover, the bound is optimal up to a constant factor. (b) Next, for $m$-edge graph $G$ with $\\lambda (G) > \\sqrt{(1-\\frac{1}{r})2m}$, we show that it must contain $\\Omega_r (\\sqrt{m})$ copies of $K_{r+1}$ sharing $r$ common vertices. This confirms a conjecture of Li, Liu and Feng and unifies a series of spectral extremal results on books and cliques. Moreover, we also show that such a graph $G$ contains $\\Omega_r (m^{\\frac{r-1}{2}})$ copies of $K_{r+1}$. This extends a result of Ning and Zhai for counting triangles. (c) We prove that every $m$-edge graph $G$ with $\\lambda (G) > \\sqrt{m}$ contains at least $(\\frac{1}{8}-o(1)) m^2$ copies of 4-cycles, and we provide two constructions showing that the constant $\\frac{1}{8}$ is the best possible. This result settles a problem raised by Ning and Zhai, and it gives the first asymptotics for counting degenerate bipartite graphs. The key to our proof are two structural results we obtain for graphs with large spectral radii on their maximum degree and on existence of large structured subgraphs, which we believe to be of independent interest.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\ne-Positive caterpillars have maximum degree at most three (https://arxiv.cnoctave.top/pdf/2610.39481)\nciteturn4search17 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 days ago; This includes unit interval graphs and familiar examples such as *complete graphs, paths, twinned paths, lariats, lollipops, melting lollipops, twinned lollipops, dumbbells, generalized bulls, K-chains,* and *triangular ladders*. Beyond this setting, Tom and Vailaya [29, Cors. 54 and 65] proved e-positivity for vertex-glued sequences of naturally labeled unit interval graphs and cycles, and for their cyclic closures, encompassing *cycles, tadpoles, hats, adjacent cycle-chains* (aka. *hat chains*), *kayak paddles, clique-path-cycle graphs, twinned cycles, cycle-chords,* and *sequential cycle-chords*. ... Further examples include infinite non-e-positive families in each class of (claw, \\(H\\))-free graphs with \\(H \\in \\{\\text{diamond}, C_4, K_4, 2K_2\\}\\), *brooms* other than paths and the spider \\(S(2,1,1)\\), and *double brooms* that do not degenerate to brooms; see Foley, Hoàng, and Merkel [10] and Wang and Wang [31].\n2                D.Q.B. TANG, D.G.L. WANG, AND M.M.Y. WANG\n\nOn the positive side, Hikita [13] proved the *Stanley–Stembridge conjecture* [27], establishing e-positivity for incomparability graphs of (3 + 1)-free posets. This includes unit interval graphs and familiar examples such as *complete graphs, paths, twinned paths, lariats, lollipops, melting lollipops, twinned lollipops, dumbbells, generalized bulls, K-chains,* and *triangular ladders*. Beyond this setting, Tom and Vailaya [29, Cors. 54 and 65] proved e-positivity for vertex-glued sequences of naturally labeled unit interval graphs and cycles, and for their cyclic closures, encompassing *cycles, tadpoles, hats, adjacent cycle-chains* (aka. *hat chains*), *kayak paddles, clique-path-cycle graphs, twinned cycles, cycle-chords,* and *sequential cycle-chords*. Further instances outside the results above include line graphs of tadpoles, spiders of the form \\(S(m+1,m,1)\\), and *clocks*, see Wang and Wang [32], Dahlberg, Foley, and van Willigenburg [8, p. 2681], and Cheng, He, and Wang [5], respectively. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\ndegree r, for an absolute constant c. Another remarkable and still unresolved conjecture of Babai [18] states that there is an absolute constant c such that for any group G the diameter of any Cayley graph for G is bounded above by (log |G|)^c. (https://www.combinatorics.org/files/Surveys/ds14/ds14v2-2013.pdf)\nciteturn4search18 [wordlim: 200] Published: 13.4 years ago; Another remarkable and still unresolved conjecture of Babai [18] states that there is an absolute constant c such that for any group G the diameter of any Cayley graph for G is bounded above by (log |G|)^c. ... Investigations of large abelian Cayley graphs of given size of reduced generating set and given diameter can be based on the following simple but ingenious idea (see [132] for genesis and background).\ndegree r, for an absolute constant c. Another remarkable and still unresolved conjecture of Babai [18] states that there is an absolute constant c such that for any group G the diameter of any Cayley graph for G is bounded above by (log |G|)^c.\n\nIn terms of computer generation, note that roughly one half of the values in Table 2 have been obtained from Cayley graphs. A description of computer-assisted constructions of large (Δ, D)-graphs (for manageable values of Δ and D) from Cayley graphs of semidirect products of (mostly cyclic) groups can be found in Hafner [200]. Later, Branković et al. [72] showed that the constructions of [200] can be obtained as lifts of smaller Cayley graphs with voltage assignments in smaller, mostly cyclic, groups. Researchers who have contributed in the quest for large Cayley graphs of given degree and diameter also include Campbell [82], and Akers and Krishnamurthy [6].\n\nTo conclude on a more general note, it is worth mentioning that Lakshmivarahan, Jwo and Dhall [231] produced a survey of Cayley graph network designs. Apart from the usual properties of order, degree and diameter, they also consider shortest path distance, vertex-transitivity, arc-transitivity and several forms of distance transitivity. The survey emphasises algebraic features, such as cosets, conjugacy classes, and automorphism actions, in the determination of some topological properties of over 18 types of networks. Cayley graphs have been used numerous times as a tool for the design and analysis of interconnection networks, for an example see Schibell and Stafford [301].\n\n### 3.4.3  Abelian Cayley graphs\n\nFurther restrictions on the classes of groups yield better upper bounds. We discuss here in more detail the Cayley graphs of abelian groups. Let AC\\_{\\Delta,D} denote the largest order of a Cayley graph of an abelian group of degree Δ and diameter D. Inequalities for such graphs are often stated in terms of the number of generators of the reduced generating set rather than the degree. Given a Cayley graph C(Γ, S), the reduced generating set is a subset S′ of S such that, for each s ∈ S, exactly one of s, s⁻¹ appears in S′. If the reduced generating set has d elements then the degree of the Cayley graph is equal to Δ = 2d − d′, where d′ is the number of generators of order two in S.\n\nInvestigations of large abelian Cayley graphs of given size of reduced generating set and given diameter can be based on the following simple but ingenious idea (see [132] for genesis and background). Any finite abelian group Γ with a symmetric generating set S and a reduced generating set S′ = {g₁, . . . , g_d} of size d is a quotient group of the free abelian d-generator group ℤ^d by the subgroup N (of finite index), that is, the kernel of the natural homomorphism ℤ^d → Γ, which sends the unit vector e_i ∈ ℤ^d onto g_i. For any given D, define\n\nW_{d,D} = {(x₁, . . . , x_d) ∈ ℤ^d; |x₁| + . . . + |x_d| ≤ D}.\n\nThen the Cayley graph C(Γ, S) has diameter at most D if and only if W_{d,D} + N = ℤ^d.\n\nTHE ELECTRONIC JOURNAL OF COMBINATORICS 20(2) (2013), #DS14v2 29--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(PDF) Spectral supersaturation: Triangles and bowties (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/382080258_Spectral_supersaturation_Triangles_and_bowties)\nciteturn4search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.3 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; Inspired by this result, we conjecture that the number of ... a complete bipartite graph K a,b by embedding two disjoint edges into the vertex part of ... studied the spectral supersaturation for 4-cycles by showing that #C 4≥1\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\ndegree \\(r\\), for an absolute constant \\(c\\). Another remarkable and still unresolved conjecture of Babai [18] states that there is an absolute constant \\(c\\) such that for any group \\(G\\) the diameter of any Cayley graph for \\(G\\) is bounded a